hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in problema one of fear and trembling  Abraham is going to make a vital  distinction between Abraham understood  as the father of faith in this solitary  figure and the tragic hero now  one might be tempted to read this as a  opposition between Athens and Jerusalem  philosophy and faith or Greek culture  and you know Hebrew literature and  culture and that would be completely  wrong why would that be wrong it's not a  clash between Christianity and paganism  because one of the figures who he's  going to discuss as a tragic hero is  somebody right out of the Old Testament  one of the judges Jeptha know the fault  line here runs in a different direction  and before we go on let me mention that  the three heroes that he's talking about  in this section are taken from Greek  Roman and Hebrew literature and history  right  Agamemnon Jeptha and Brutus why pick  those three you might say that  Kierkegaard is covering all of the bases  of classic antiquity the things that  flowed most steadily not exclusively but  but had the greatest impact upon the  development of Western thought so by  addressing them he is like I said  covering his bases he also mentions  Shakespeare showing us that somebody  who's more contemporary is dealing with  tragic heroes and he says why didn't  Shakespeare have any characters like  Abraham in them later on he's also going  to discuss Socrates and how Socrates  functions as a sort of tragic hero less  in action and more in the realm of  thought but of course sacrificing his  own life for philosophy so how are the  tragic heroes  different from somebody like Abraham  this is connected with this notion of  teleological suspension of the ethical  and it's very useful for us to think in  terms of these stages or perspectives  that Kierkegaard sets out in relation to  each other the aesthetic is not going to  be discussed very much here that is the  realm of particular individuals and they  aren't recognising the universal and the  way that the ethical is instead they're  living their lives and maybe making  money enjoying themselves indulging  themselves in certain things pursuing  certain Telos tell you know tell a ends  goals purposes but those that are  germane to themselves and when we find  something as we say bigger than  ourselves that we belong to that helps  place things into context for us whether  that be something intellectual whether  that be something involved in action  whether that be a cause we find  ourselves in the realm of what  Kierkegaard is calling the ethical  there's something Universal that we  recognize and Abraham has this for  Abraham as Kierkegaard points out the  ethical would be his family and indeed  the promise that God is made of many  nations being blessed through him but  it's primarily the small community that  is his family as a set of individuals in  relation to each other he's supposed to  love them that is part of the ethical  for for him now why would we bring up  tragic heroes it's possible to  distinguish levels within the ethical so  it's not just a matter of the religious  versus the ethical versus the aesthetic  there are higher and lower we might say  levels each with their own Telos their  own purpose their own goal that can be  recognized within the ethical and some  of our moral developments and a lot of  our moral  conflicts in fact depend upon this so he  says the tragic hero is still within the  ethical he allows an expression of the  ethical to have its Telos in a higher  expression of the ethical that's a very  important point there and the reason why  Kierkegaard actually brings up these  three guys Agamemnon Jephthah and Brutus  is because each one of them does an act  similar in description to what Abraham  is contemplating doing with Isaac namely  sacrifices or kills their own child why  would somebody kill their child we might  think of all sorts of criminal reasons  that a person might might kill their  child for say you know think about the  case of Medea in antiquity where she  essentially is killing her children to  spite her husband who is cheating on her  and this is looked at as a horrible act  by everybody in antiquity and the only  debate about is how culpable is she in  doing this and how much blame does Jason  bear but it's understood as a violation  of the ethical could there be situations  in which one actually does need to  sacrifice one's child and God says yes  there can be a higher expression of the  ethical he says the tragic hero scales  down the ethical relation between father  and son or daughter and father to a  feeling that has its dialectic in its  relation to the idea of moral conduct  what is that idea of moral conduct this  is something that is needed for the  larger community and in the case of  Agamemnon it has to do with the ship's  being able to sail to Troy there's this  entire fabric of Greek society that is  dependent upon the sacrifice of this  unfortunate person Jeptha he makes a  rash vow to God in order to win victory  for the people of Israel against their  oppressors then he will sacrifice the  first thing that comes  to meet him when he gets home turns out  that it's his daughter Brutus has a son  who doesn't do what he's supposed to do  and rather does the opposite and has to  be executed by his own father who  doesn't and these are viewed as a higher  expression of the ethical there is still  some Telos some point within it and it  has to do with the good of the community  so the tragic hero is indeed somebody  who is in some sense doing the right  thing and and notice what he says about  this when in the crucial moment  Agamemnon Jephthah  and Brutus heroically have overcome the  agony heroically have lost the beloved  they love their children but they they  recognize that they have to kill them  they have only to complete the task  externally there will never be a noble  soul in the world without tears of  compassion for their agony of admiration  for their deed he says what they don't  do is what Abraham does what does  Abraham do this is part of it  he says if in the crucial moment these  three men were to append to the heroic  courage with which they bore the agony  the little phrase but it will not happen  anyway who then would understand them if  they went on to explain this we believed  by virtue of the absurd who would  understand them any better yet that is  what Abraham is according to Kierkegaard  doing Abraham has faith in God that  somehow by virtue of the absurd this is  all going to work out that anything is  possible for God and he believes that  God is in fact demanding the sacrifice  of his child not for a community but for  some thing beyond the ethical so in the  case of the the tragic hero  the person who sacrifices for the good  of their community what they do is  understandable it moves within the realm  of what Kierkegaard calls the you  reversal any one of us we don't want to  step into their place but we could if we  actually possess the courage and the  moral fiber that they have we could do  that and it would make sense  likewise it could be Socrates allowing  the city of Athens to execute him and  choosing not to flee his prison cell  when the guards have been bribed so that  he can take a stand for the practice of  philosophy and the pursuit of wisdom  again there's some sort of universal  human value there and we might think of  countless tragic heroes in our own time  doing similar things recognizing that  they may in fact fail but taking on  those challenges anyway in ways that we  can understand recognize replicates  perhaps so that's the realm of the  tragic hero that's the realm of the  ethical Abraham goes beyond that he is  involved in what Kierkegaard points to  is a higher Telos what is the higher  tellest in this case he tells us in the  text that it is fulfilling the will of  God now some people might view the will  of God as something that is just within  the realm of the ethical and quite often  within not just paganism but within both  Judaism and Christianity that's how it's  understood Kierkegaard will actually say  that a church is really no different  than a state in this respect but there  will be some individuals and he uses  Abraham he also talks about Mary and he  talks about Jesus and His disciples for  whom there is a higher call a higher  call that introduces a greater Telos  that has to be answered to and which  does not provide you with a  justification that other people will be  able to understand because there is the  absurd in  auld there so he says Abraham situation  is a case in which he transgresses the  ethical altogether and had a higher  Telos outside of it he goes on and he  says that why does Abraham do it for  God's sake and the two are wholly  identical for his own sake he does it  for God's sake  because God demands this proof of his  faith he does it for his own sake so he  can prove it and that is as much  explanation as we're really going to get  so what else do we find here gagarin  will talk about he brings this this  catch phrase up over and over again the  anxiety to distress the paradox that is